---
import ArticlePage from "../../components/ArticlePage.astro";
import NuclideRef from "../../components/NuclideRef.astro";
import { luExamples, LU_DOSE_GBQ } from "../../lib/note-examples";
import { pct1 } from "../../lib/guide-tables";
import { prefillPath } from "../../lib/restore";
import { u } from "../../lib/site";

/** ★ Lu-177 풀이 — 노리는 검색어: lu 177 decay calculator(노출 22 · 순위 60, 맞는 쪽이 없었다).
 *  ★ 표의 % 는 `/radiopharmaceutical-decay/#lu-177` 표와 **같은 함수**(`decayGridDays`)의 칸이다. 시험이 그 일치를 잰다.
 *  ★ ±10% 는 미국 라벨(16절)의 공급 규격이고 EMA 정보에는 없다(2026-11-01 원문 대조). 이 사이트의 규정도 투여 허용 범위도 아니다 — 글은 예시로만 쓴다.
 *  ★ 활성도 창은 사용 가능 시간이 아니다 — 유효기간 72시간(두 라벨 모두)을 글이 함께 말한다.
 *  ★ 투약 지시로 읽히지 않게 쓴다 — 예제의 값은 산수의 예시다. */
const X = luExamples();
const gbq2 = (x: number) => x.toFixed(2);
const thursday = prefillPath("decay", { nuclide: "Lu-177", a0: X.ordered, unit: "GBq", t: 3, tu: "d" });
const reach = prefillPath("decay", { mode: "when", nuclide: "Lu-177", a0: X.ordered, unit: "GBq", target: LU_DOSE_GBQ });
const week = prefillPath("decay", { nuclide: "Lu-177", a0: LU_DOSE_GBQ, unit: "GBq", t: 7, tu: "d" });
const fortnight = prefillPath("decay", { nuclide: "Lu-177", a0: LU_DOSE_GBQ, unit: "GBq", t: 14, tu: "d" });
const d7 = X.days.find((r) => r.d === 7)!;
const d14 = X.days.find((r) => r.d === 14)!;
---
<ArticlePage slug="lu-177-decay-worked-example">
  <p>
    A <NuclideRef k="Lu-177" /> therapy dose is prescribed for the moment of infusion, but it is calibrated
    days earlier and may sit in a vial for days afterwards. This note follows one dose of
    {" "}{LU_DOSE_GBQ}&nbsp;GBq ({X.mCi.toFixed(0)}&nbsp;mCi) from calibration to storage: how much activity has
    to be there at the start, what is in the vial on the day, what a delay costs, and what is left a week or
    two later. The percentages in the two tables below are entries of the
    {" "}<a href={u("/radiopharmaceutical-decay/#lu-177")} class="underline">Lu-177 remaining-activity table</a>,
    and any other interval can be run in the <a href={u("/decay/")} class="underline">decay calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Which half-life, and what it means per day</h2>
  <p>
    This site uses {X.tDays} days ({X.tHours.toFixed(2)} hours), the evaluated value in the IAEA Livechart.
    European product information prints 6.647 days. Over 14 days the two differ by
    {" "}{X.labelGap14Pct.toFixed(2)}%, so the choice does not matter for planning, as long as one value is used
    throughout.
  </p>
  <p>
    The shortcut to avoid is “a half-life is about a week”. After 7 days, {pct1(d7.f)}% is left, not 50%.
    A more useful pair of figures for checking arithmetic in your head: Lu-177 loses
    {" "}{X.perDayPct.toFixed(1)}% in the first day and {X.perHourPct.toFixed(2)}% in the first hour.
  </p>

  <h2>How much activity is needed at calibration</h2>
  <p>
    Going back in time means dividing. If {LU_DOSE_GBQ}&nbsp;GBq is wanted at infusion and t is the time from
    calibration to infusion, the calibrated activity is {LU_DOSE_GBQ} / 2<sup>−t/T½</sup>:
  </p>
  <div class="not-prose my-3 pin-first table-fit" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Calibration to infusion</th><th class="text-right">Remaining</th><th class="text-right">Needed at calibration</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {X.dispatch.map((r) => (
          <tr><td class="num">{r.h}&nbsp;h</td><td class="num text-right">{pct1(r.f)}%</td><td class="num text-right">{gbq2(r.gbq)}&nbsp;GBq</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    A ready-to-use product may already be labelled for the infusion time: the European information for
    lutetium oxodotreotide states 7&nbsp;400&nbsp;MBq at the date and time of infusion, from a fixed
    370&nbsp;MBq/mL at calibration with the volume set to suit. In that case the correction runs from the time on
    that label. The table is for a pharmacy working from a bulk calibration or checking a supplier's figure,
    and it is decay arithmetic only: the same information limits that product to 72 hours from calibration,
    so a row in this table is not a statement that a preparation can still be used that late.
  </p>

  <h2>Monday's calibration, Thursday's infusion</h2>
  <p>
    A vial is labelled {X.ordered}&nbsp;GBq at 10:00 on Monday. The infusion is booked for 10:00 on Thursday,
    with {LU_DOSE_GBQ}&nbsp;GBq prescribed. What should the vial hold?
  </p>
  <ol class="my-3 list-decimal space-y-1 pl-5">
    <li>Elapsed time: 3 days exactly.</li>
    <li>Half-lives: 3 / {X.tDays} = {X.n3.toFixed(4)}.</li>
    <li>Remaining fraction: 2<sup>−{X.n3.toFixed(4)}</sup> = {X.dispatch[2].f.toFixed(4)}, the {pct1(X.dispatch[2].f)}% of the table's 3&nbsp;d row.</li>
    <li>Activity: {X.ordered} × {X.dispatch[2].f.toFixed(4)} = <strong>{gbq2(X.atThursday)}&nbsp;GBq</strong>, which is {X.atThursdayPct.toFixed(1)}% of the prescription.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <a href={u(thursday)} class="underline">Open the calculator with these inputs</a> to see the same result.
    Treat that figure as what the dose calibrator ought to show before administration. The calibrator
    reading is the measurement; the calculation tells you whether it looks right.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same vial answers the opposite question. <a href={u(reach)} class="underline">The “When does it reach”
    mode</a>, with {X.ordered}&nbsp;GBq as the start and {LU_DOSE_GBQ}&nbsp;GBq as the target, gives
    {" "}{(X.reachH / 24).toFixed(3)} days, or {X.reachH.toFixed(1)} hours, which is about {X.reachClock} on Thursday. Before that moment the vial
    holds more than the prescription, and afterwards less.
  </p>

  <h2>Drawing from a bulk vial</h2>
  <p>
    Decay lowers the concentration while the volume stays where it was, so a dose drawn from bulk solution
    needs more millilitres as the days go by. Suppose the bulk is {X.bulk.conc0.toFixed(1)}&nbsp;GBq/mL at
    Monday's calibration. On Thursday the concentration is {X.bulk.conc0.toFixed(1)} × {X.dispatch[2].f.toFixed(4)}
    {" "}= {X.bulk.concNow.toFixed(3)}&nbsp;GBq/mL, and {LU_DOSE_GBQ}&nbsp;GBq takes
    {" "}<strong>{X.bulk.volNow.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;mL</strong>. Drawing the {X.bulk.vol0.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;mL that
    Monday's concentration suggests would deliver {X.bulk.wrongGBq.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;GBq, {X.bulk.wrongPct.toFixed(1)}%
    short. A calculated volume is only a starting point; the syringe still goes in the calibrator.
  </p>

  <h2>What a delay costs</h2>
  <p>
    Take a vial holding exactly {LU_DOSE_GBQ}&nbsp;GBq at the planned time. If the patient is two hours late it
    holds {gbq2(X.twoHoursLate)}&nbsp;GBq, {X.twoHoursLatePct.toFixed(2)}% less. If treatment moves to the next day
    it holds {gbq2(X.dayLate)}&nbsp;GBq, {X.dayLatePct.toFixed(1)}% less.
  </p>
  <p>
    To see how far that goes, borrow a tolerance. The US label for lutetium Lu 177 dotatate supplies the vial at
    {" "}{LU_DOSE_GBQ}&nbsp;GBq ±&nbsp;10% at the time of injection, which makes the limits {gbq2(X.low)} and
    {" "}{gbq2(X.high)}&nbsp;GBq for this example. Solving t&nbsp;=&nbsp;T½·log₂(A₀/A) for each one, the activity
    falls through the upper limit {X.earlyH.toFixed(1)} hours before the planned time and through the lower
    limit {X.lateH.toFixed(1)} hours after it, a span of about {(X.earlyH + X.lateH).toFixed(0)} hours, and the
    next-day case above only just stays inside.
  </p>
  <p>
    That span describes activity and nothing more. It does not mean a dose can be moved by that much: the same
    label gives a shelf life of 72 hours from calibration, which the Monday-to-Thursday example above reaches
    exactly, and a supply specification is not a dosing tolerance. Whether to delay or reschedule is decided by
    the product label, your protocol and the treating team, not by this arithmetic.
  </p>
  <p>
    Clocks deserve a separate check. European labelling prints these times in UTC. Read as local time, a UTC time
    is nine hours out at UTC+9: calibration really happened later than you assumed, so the vial holds
    {" "}{X.utc9AbovePct.toFixed(1)}% more than the calculation says. At UTC−5 the error runs the other way, five
    hours and {X.utc5BelowPct.toFixed(1)}% less. The larger of those would use more than a third of a ±10% band
    before anything else has gone wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>The vial after 7 and 14 days</h2>
  <p>
    A cancelled dose stays radioactive for a long time. These are the same table rows applied to
    {" "}{LU_DOSE_GBQ}&nbsp;GBq:
  </p>
  <div class="not-prose my-3 pin-first table-fit" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Days after the {LU_DOSE_GBQ}&nbsp;GBq time</th><th class="text-right">Remaining</th><th class="text-right">Activity</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {X.days.map((r) => (
          <tr><td class="num">{r.d}</td><td class="num text-right">{pct1(r.f)}%</td><td class="num text-right">{gbq2(r.gbq)}&nbsp;GBq</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    So <a href={u(week)} class="underline">one week leaves {gbq2(d7.gbq)}&nbsp;GBq</a> and
    {" "}<a href={u(fortnight)} class="underline">two weeks leave {gbq2(d14.gbq)}&nbsp;GBq</a>. These figures
    describe a vial and nothing else. Activity in a patient also falls through excretion, so it drops faster
    than physical decay alone, and this calculation does not model that.
  </p>

  <h2>When Lu-177m decides storage</h2>
  <p>
    Ten half-lives of Lu-177 take {X.tenDays.toFixed(1)} days and reduce {LU_DOSE_GBQ}&nbsp;GBq to
    {" "}{X.tenLeftMBq.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;MBq. Whether that ends the matter depends on how the Lu-177 was made. Product
    information names two starting materials, lutetium-176 and ytterbium-176, with different waste management.
    Material made directly from Lu-176 can carry the isomer <NuclideRef k="Lu-177m" />, half-life
    {" "}{X.isoHalfLifeD} days. Over those same {X.tenDays.toFixed(1)} days the isomer only falls to
    {" "}{X.isoLeftPct.toFixed(1)}%, while Lu-177 on its own has fallen below 0.1%. Waste that looks finished by
    the Lu-177 arithmetic may not be.
  </p>
  <p>
    The decay calculator follows one nuclide at a time, so it will not show you this mix, nor the part of the
    isomer's decay that feeds back into Lu-177 by isomeric transition. Run Lu-177m as its own calculation, starting
    from the content your supplier states, read the supplier's documentation on waste, and apply the clearance
    rules that hold where you work. The <NuclideRef k="Lu-177" /> sheet describes the two production routes.
  </p>
</ArticlePage>
